---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Policy"
  description="How K-Beauty Truth separates personal use, research, and brand claims."
  canonicalPath="/editorial-policy/"
>
  <section class="stack-page">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1 class="page-title">Editorial Policy</h1>
      <p>
        {site.name} publishes personal skincare notes. The standard is simple: do not invent use,
        and do not dress a personal reaction up as a universal fact.
      </p>

      <h2>Used versus researched</h2>
      <p>
        A review is a used-product note only when Kay bought the product and used it. If a page
        includes background that did not come from her own use, that will be written as research or
        as a brand claim, not as something her skin did.
      </p>

      <h2>How products arrive</h2>
      <p>
        Direct purchases are the default. If a brand sends a product, that will be disclosed on the
        page. Affiliate links, when they exist, are disclosed on the page and in the
        <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Personal experience versus brand language</h2>
      <p>
        Ingredient lists, official names, and marketing copy belong to the brand. Kay’s notes
        belong to her face. Those two things are not treated as the same kind of evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>Updates and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a routine changes, a product is finished, or a factual error is found, the page should
        be updated. Dates are added only when they are known. Missing timeframes stay missing.
      </p>

      <h2>Skin is not a sample size of one</h2>
      <p>
        A breakout, a repurchase, or a calm week is what happened to Kay. Another person can have
        the opposite result with the same bottle.
      </p>

      <h2>Not medical advice</h2>
      <p>
        These pages are not a diagnosis, treatment, or substitute for a licensed clinician. Kay is
        not a dermatologist or other medical professional.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
